\begin_layout Section

\lang american
Concentration of measure inequalities
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\lang american
Markow's inequality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\lang american
Statement
\series default
:
 For any non-negative stochastic variable 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 and with 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon>0$
\end_inset

 we have 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}(X\geq\epsilon)\leq\frac{\mathbb{E}[X]}{\epsilon}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\lang american
Proof
\series default
:
 Define 
\begin_inset Formula $Y:=1(X\geq\epsilon)$
\end_inset

 then 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\leq\frac{X}{\epsilon}$
\end_inset

 (if 
\begin_inset Formula $0\leq X<\epsilon$
\end_inset

 then 
\begin_inset Formula $Y=0\leq\frac{X}{\epsilon}$
\end_inset

 and if 
\begin_inset Formula $X\geq\epsilon\iff\frac{X}{\epsilon}\geq1$
\end_inset

 then 
\begin_inset Formula $Y=1\leq\frac{X}{\epsilon}$
\end_inset

) therefore 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{P}(X\geq\epsilon)=\mathbb{P}(Y=1)=\mathbb{E}[Y]\leq\mathbb{E}[\frac{X}{\epsilon}]=\frac{\mathbb{E}[X]}{\epsilon}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
This can be rewritten by defining 
\begin_inset Formula $a=\frac{\epsilon}{\mathbb{E}[X]}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}[X]\neq0$
\end_inset

) and we have 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}(X\geq a\mathbb{E}[X])\leq\frac{1}{a}.
\]

\end_inset

That is,
 the probability of an event 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 times away from the stochastic variables mean value is decaying with 
\begin_inset Formula $1/a$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\lang american
Another (simpler) proof
\series default
:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}\left[X\right] & =\mathbb{E}\left[X\mathbb{I}_{(X\geq\epsilon)}+X\mathbb{I}_{(X<\epsilon)}\right]\\
 & =\mathbb{E}\left[X\mathbb{I}_{(X\geq\epsilon)}\right]+\underbrace{\mathbb{E}\left[X\mathbb{I}_{(X<\epsilon)}\right]}_{\geq0\text{ (X is non negative)}}\\
 & \geq\mathbb{E}\left[X\mathbb{I}_{(X\geq\epsilon)}\right]\\
 & =\int^{\infty}_{\epsilon}x\mathbb{P}\left(X=x\right)\,dx\qquad\left(\sum_{x\geq\epsilon}x\mathbb{P}\left(X=x\right)\right)\\
 & \geq\int^{\infty}_{\epsilon}\epsilon\mathbb{P}\left(X=x\right)\,dx\qquad\left(\sum_{x\geq\epsilon}\epsilon\mathbb{P}\left(X=x\right)\right)\\
 & =\epsilon\mathbb{P}\left(X\geq\epsilon\right).
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Rearranging yields Markow's inequality
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}\left(X\geq\epsilon\right)\geq\frac{\mathbb{E}\left[X\right]}{\epsilon}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\lang american
Chebychew's inequality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\lang american
Statement
\series default
:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}(|X-\mathbb{E}[X]|\geq\epsilon)\leq\frac{\mathbb{V}(X)}{\epsilon^{2}}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\lang american
Proof
\series default
:
 For any 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon>0$
\end_inset

 we have (by Markow's inequality) 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}((X-\mathbb{E}[X])^{2}\geq\epsilon^{2})\leq\frac{\mathbb{E}[(X-\mathbb{E}[X])^{2}]}{\epsilon^{2}}=\frac{\mathbb{V}(X)}{\epsilon^{2}}-
\]

\end_inset

Finally we note that 
\series bold

\begin_inset Formula 
\[
|X-\mathbb{E}[X]|\geq\epsilon\iff(X-\mathbb{E}[X])^{2}\geq\epsilon^{2}
\]

\end_inset

 
\series default
and thus
\series bold
 
\series default

\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{P}(|X-\mathbb{E}[X]|\geq\epsilon)\leq\frac{\mathbb{V}(X)}{\epsilon^{2}}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\lang american
Hoeffding's inequality
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\lang american
Statement
\series default
:
 Given the independent stochastic variables 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{1},\dots,X_{n}$
\end_inset

 where there exists 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i,}b_{i}$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in\{1,\dots,n\}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}\in[a_{i},b_{i}]$
\end_inset

.
 For all 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon>0$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}\left(\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}-\mathbb{E}\left[\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}\right]\geq\epsilon\right)\leq\exp\left\{ -\frac{2\epsilon^{2}}{\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}}\right\} .
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\lang american
Proof
\series default
:
 Start by simple rewriting and application of Markow's inequality - which is legal since 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{X}\equiv\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}$
\end_inset

 are composed of i.i.d.
 stochastic variables and thus 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{X}-\texttt{constant}$
\end_inset

 itself is a stochastic variable (here 
\begin_inset Formula $\texttt{constant}=\mathbb{E}\left(\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}\right)$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{P}\left(\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}-\mathbb{E}\left[\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}\right]\geq\epsilon\right) & =\mathbb{P}\left(e^{\lambda\left(\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}-\mathbb{E}\left[\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}\right]\right)}\geq e^{\lambda\epsilon}\right)\\
 & \leq\frac{E\left[e^{\lambda\left(\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}-\mathbb{E}\left[\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}\right]\right)}\right]}{e^{\lambda\epsilon}}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
The nominator can be reduced.
 Firstly we use that the expectation operator is linear so that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}\left[\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}\right]=\sum^{n}_{i=1}\mathbb{E}[X_{i}]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}\left[e^{\lambda\left(\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}-\mathbb{E}\left[\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}\right]\right)}\right] & =\mathbb{E}\left[e^{\lambda\left(\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}-\sum^{n}_{i=1}\mathbb{E}[X_{i}]\right)}\right]\\
 & =\mathbb{E}\left[e^{\lambda\sum^{n}_{i=1}(X_{i}-\mathbb{E}[X_{i}])}\right].
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Now using the properties of the exponential function and Hoeffdings lemma
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}\left[e^{\lambda\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}-\mathbb{E}[X_{i}]}\right] & =\mathbb{E}\left[\prod^{n}_{i=1}e^{\lambda(X_{i}-\mathbb{E}[X_{i}])}\right]\\
 & \overset{indep.}{=}\prod^{n}_{i=1}E\left[e^{\lambda(X_{i}-\mathbb{E}[X_{i}])}\right]\\
 & \leq\prod^{n}_{i=1}e^{\lambda^{2}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}/8}\\
 & =e^{\lambda^{2}/8\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Taking stock:
 so far we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}\left(\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}-\mathbb{E}\left[\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}\right]\geq\epsilon\right)\leq e^{\lambda^{2}/8\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}-\lambda\epsilon}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
However we want the tightest probability bound,
 and therefore we minimize the right hand side expression wrt.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda>0.$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\lambda^{*}=\text{arg }\min_{\lambda\in\mathbb{R}_{+}}\enspace e^{\lambda^{2}/8\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}-\lambda\epsilon}=\text{arg }\min_{\lambda\in\mathbb{R}_{+}}\,\left\{ \frac{\lambda^{2}}{8}\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}-\lambda\epsilon\right\} ,
\]

\end_inset

with necessary first order condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
0=\frac{\lambda^{*}}{4}\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}-\epsilon\iff\lambda^{*}=\frac{4\epsilon}{\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Substituting this into the stock-taking expression yields the theorem statement
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
e^{(\lambda^{*})^{2}/8\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}-\lambda^{*}\epsilon} & =\exp\left\{ \left(\frac{4\epsilon}{\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}}.\right)^{2}\frac{1}{8}\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}-\frac{4\epsilon^{2}}{\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}}\right\} \\
 & =\exp\left\{ \frac{2\epsilon^{2}}{\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}}-\frac{4\epsilon^{2}}{\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}}\right\} \\
 & =\exp\left\{ -\frac{2\epsilon^{2}}{\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}}\right\} .
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Taken all together we have obtained Hoeffding Inequality:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}\left(\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}-\mathbb{E}\left[\sum^{n}_{i=1}X_{i}\right]\geq\epsilon\right)\leq\exp\left\{ -\frac{2\epsilon^{2}}{\sum^{n}_{i=1}(b_{i}-a_{i})^{2}}\right\} .
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section

\lang american
Generalization bounds:
 Finite and Countably Infinity Hypothesis Classes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\lang american
Single hypothesis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\lang american
Setting and risk definitions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Assume the loss function 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell(h(X),Y)$
\end_inset

 is bounded on 
\begin_inset Formula $[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
 This is important for the specific calculations but the general insights are applicable to any loss function bounds 
\begin_inset Formula $[a,b]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\lang american
True risk
\series default
:

\series bold
 
\series default
The quality of any hypothesis 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 is measured by its (true) risk
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L(h)\equiv\mathbb{E}_{(X,Y)\sim p}\left[\ell(h(X),Y)\right],
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
where 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 is the 
\emph on
unknown
\emph default
 distribution over which the world has generated 
\begin_inset Formula $(X,Y)$
\end_inset

 - i.e.
 pairs of features from the input space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{X}$
\end_inset

 and outcomes from the label space 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{Y}$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\lang american
Empirical risk
\series default
:
 Using a validation sample
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
S=\left\{ (X_{1},Y_{1}),\dots,(X_{N},Y_{N})\right\} ,\quad(X_{i},Y_{i})\overset{iid}{\sim}p,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
we calculate the empirical risk defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{L}(h,S)\equiv\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ell(h(X_{n}),Y_{n}).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\lang american
Theoretical properties of empirical risk
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\lang american
Unbiased:
 
\series default
Since the validation samples are independently and identically distributed from the distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

 we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}_{S\sim p^{N}}\left[\hat{L}(h,S)\right] & =\mathbb{E}_{S\sim p^{N}}\left[\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ell(h(X_{n}),Y_{n})\right]\\
 & =\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\mathbb{E}_{(X_{n},Y_{n})\sim p}\left[\ell(h(X_{n}),Y_{n})\right]\\
 & =\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\mathbb{E}_{(X_{1},Y_{1})\sim p}\left[\ell(h(X_{1}),Y_{1})\right]\\
 & =\mathbb{E}_{(X_{1},Y_{1})\sim p}\left[\ell(h(X_{1}),Y_{1})\right]\\
 & =L(h).
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
However the above only holds because 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

.
 If not we generally have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{E}_{S\sim p^{N}}\left[\hat{L}\left(\hat{h}_{S},S\right)\right]\neq\mathbb{E}_{S\sim p^{N}}\left[L\left(\hat{h}_{S}\right)\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\lang american
What can be said about the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{L}(h,S)$
\end_inset

?
 
\series default
Formally what is 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

 here
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}\left(L(h)-\hat{L}(h,S)\geq\epsilon\right)\leq\delta.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
I.e.
 with what tightness can we bound the probability that the true risk 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon>0$
\end_inset

 bigger than 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{L}$
\end_inset

.
 For example we might want a confindence of 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta=0.05$
\end_inset

 that is w.p.
 less than 
\begin_inset Formula $0.05$
\end_inset

 the true loss 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon=f(\delta)$
\end_inset

 - that depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta$
\end_inset

 units bigger than 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{L}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Applying Hoeffdings bound yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{P}\left(L(h)-\hat{L}(h,S)\geq\epsilon\right) & =\mathbb{P}\left(\underbrace{\mathbb{E}\left[\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ell(h(X_{n}),Y_{n})\right]}_{=\mathbb{E}\left[\ell(h(X_{1}),Y_{1})\right]=L(h)}-\frac{1}{N}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ell(h(X_{n}),Y_{n})\geq\epsilon\right)\\
 & =\mathbb{P}\left(\mathbb{E}\left[\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ell(h(X_{n}),Y_{n})\right]-\sum^{N}_{n=1}\ell(h(X_{n}),Y_{n})\geq N\epsilon\right)\\
 & \leq\exp\left\{ -\frac{2(N\epsilon)^{2}}{\sum^{N}_{n=1}(b_{n}-a_{n})^{2}}\right\} \\
 & =\exp\left\{ -2N\epsilon^{2}\right\} .
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Defining the relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta=\exp\left\{ -2N\epsilon^{2}\right\} $
\end_inset

 yields 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon=\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{P}\left(L(h)\geq\hat{L}(h,S)+\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}\right) & \leq\delta,
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
and taking the complement
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}\left(L(h)<\hat{L}(h,S)+\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}\right)=1-\mathbb{P}\left(L(h)\geq\hat{L}(h,S)+\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}\right)\geq1-\delta.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\lang american
Sidenote:
 Understanding 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon=\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Earlier we obtained
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}\left(L(h)-\hat{L}(h,S)\geq\epsilon\right)\leq\exp\left\{ -2N\epsilon^{2}\right\} .
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
we can think if this inequality as asking:
 
\emph on
If you set an error-margin 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon>0$
\end_inset

 then I tell you how confident I am that an error happens (i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $L-\hat{L}\geq\epsilon$
\end_inset

).
 
\emph default
If we instead ask the question:
 
\emph on
I want the probability that an error happens 
\begin_inset Formula $(i.e.L-\hat{L}\geq\epsilon)$
\end_inset

 to be 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta\cdot100\%$
\end_inset

 how large does the error-margin 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

 have to be?

\emph default
 The latter case is answered by relabeling the right hand side as 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta=\exp\left\{ -2N\epsilon^{2}\right\} $
\end_inset

 and then backing out what 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon$
\end_inset

 must be:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon=\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
The complement flips the question:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{P}\left(L(h)<\hat{L}(h,S)+\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}\right)\geq1-\delta,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
and asks:
 The probability that 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 is acceptable (i.e.
 smaller than 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{L}(h,S)+\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}$
\end_inset

) is at least 
\begin_inset Formula $1-\delta$
\end_inset

.
 Or:
 I am 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-\delta)\cdot100\%$
\end_inset

 certain that 
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 is smaller than 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{L}(h,S)+\sqrt{\frac{1}{2N}\ln\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right)}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

