\begin_layout Subsubsection

\lang american
The Spectral Theorem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\lang american
Statement:
 
\series default
Any real valued symmetrix matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$
\end_inset

 can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A=Q\Lambda Q',
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
where 
\begin_inset Formula $Q=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{v}_{1} & \mathbf{v}_{2} & \cdots & \mathbf{v}_{n}\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$
\end_inset

 holds 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 orthogonal real-valued eigenvectors and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda=diag(\lambda_{1},\cdots,\lambda_{n})$
\end_inset

 holds their respective eigenvalues.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\lang american
Proof
\series default
:
 Firstly we show that any real valued symmetric (i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $A=A'$
\end_inset

) matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$
\end_inset

 has at least one real-valued eigenvector 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{v}_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Then we show that 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 preserves the subspace orthogonal to 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{v}_{1}$
\end_inset

 that is 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{v}_{1}'A\mathbf{x}=0$
\end_inset

 iff 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{v}_{1}'\mathbf{x}=0$
\end_inset

 and using this fact we iteratively contruct 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 real-valued orthogonal eigenvectors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
We want to maximise the following (subject to the unit circle)
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{\mathbf{x}'\mathbf{x}=1}\mathbf{x}'A\mathbf{x},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
with the lagrangian
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{x}'A\mathbf{x}-\lambda\left(\mathbf{x}'\mathbf{x}-1\right),
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
and its first order conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{\partial\mathcal{L}}{\partial\mathbf{x}}|_{\mathbf{x}=\mathbf{x}^{*}} & =(A+A')\mathbf{x}^{*}-2\lambda\mathbf{x}^{*}=2A\mathbf{x}^{*}-2\lambda\mathbf{x}^{*}=0\\
A\mathbf{x}^{*} & =\lambda\mathbf{x}^{*},
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
where the second equality uses symmetry 
\begin_inset Formula $A=A'$
\end_inset

.
 Thus we have shown that there exists a vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{v}_{1}=\mathbf{x}^{*}$
\end_inset

 with the associated eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 From here we continue by finding the next eigenvector.
 We simply solve the same maximisation problem with the added restriction 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{v}_{1}'\mathbf{x}=0$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{\mathbf{x}'\mathbf{x}=1,\mathbf{v}_{1}'\mathbf{x}=0}\mathbf{x}'A\mathbf{x},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
with the lagrangian
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{x}'A\mathbf{x}-\lambda\left(\mathbf{x}'\mathbf{x}-1\right)-\mu\left(\mathbf{v}_{1}'\mathbf{x}\right),
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
and the first order condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A\mathbf{x}^{*}=\lambda\mathbf{x}^{*}+\mu\mathbf{v}_{1}=\lambda\mathbf{x}^{*},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
where the last inequality (i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu=0$
\end_inset

) follows from symmetry of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\lang american
To see this let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{v}_{1}'\mathbf{w}=0$
\end_inset

 then because of symmetry
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{v}_{1}'A\mathbf{w}=\left(A\mathbf{v}_{1}\right)'\mathbf{w}=\left(\lambda\mathbf{v}_{1}\right)'\mathbf{w}=\lambda\mathbf{v}_{1}'\mathbf{w}=0
\]

\end_inset

 and therefore
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\underbrace{\mathbf{v}_{1}'A\mathbf{x}^{*}}_{=0}=\lambda\underbrace{\mathbf{v}_{1}'\mathbf{x}^{*}}_{=0}+\mu\underbrace{\mathbf{v}_{1}'\mathbf{v}_{1}}_{=1}\iff\mu=0.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset

 Therefore we have found another eigenvector which we name 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{v}_{2}$
\end_inset

 and its associated eigenvalue 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Keep going like this and find all 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 eigenvectors to obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A\mathbf{v}_{i}=\lambda_{i}\mathbf{v}_{i},\:i=1,\dots,n,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
which allows us to write
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
AQ=Q\Lambda,
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
where 
\begin_inset Formula $Q=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{v}_{1} & \mathbf{v}_{2} & \cdots & \mathbf{v}_{n}\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\Lambda=diag(\lambda_{1},\cdots,\lambda_{n})$
\end_inset

.
 Now remember that the eigenvectors are constructed iterately where the 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

th eigenvector is orthogonal to the preceeding 
\begin_inset Formula $j-1$
\end_inset

 eigenvectors.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{v}_{i}'\mathbf{v}_{j}=\begin{cases}
1 & \text{if }i=j\\
0 & \text{if }i\neq j
\end{cases},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
which can be collected into a matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $Q=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{v}_{1}' & \mathbf{v}_{2}' & \cdots & \mathbf{v}_{n}'\end{bmatrix}$
\end_inset

 such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
QQ' & =\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{v}_{1}\\
\mathbf{v}_{2}\\
\vdots\\
\mathbf{v}_{n}
\end{bmatrix}\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{v}_{1} & \mathbf{v}_{2} & \cdots & \mathbf{v}_{n}\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \cdots & 0\\
0 & 1 & \cdots & 0\\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots\\
0 & 0 & \cdots & 1
\end{bmatrix}=I_{n}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Therefore we have 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
AQ=Q\Lambda\iff A=Q\Lambda Q'
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
thus completing the proof.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\lang american
Overidentified case:
 
\begin_inset Formula $N>rank(X)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
Let the design matrix be defined as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X=\begin{bmatrix}\phi(\mathbf{x}_{1})' & 1\\
\vdots & \vdots\\
\phi(\mathbf{x}_{n})' & 1
\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times d+1},
\]

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi(\mathbf{x}_{i})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$
\end_inset

 is observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\dots,N$
\end_inset

 which is possibly transformed / constructed from the raw data.
 That is the raw data for person / observation 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is used to extract the features 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi(\mathbf{x}_{i})\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$
\end_inset

.
 For example we could have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\phi(\begin{bmatrix}age_{i} & height_{i}\end{bmatrix}')=\begin{bmatrix}age_{i} & age^{2}_{i} & height_{i}\end{bmatrix}',
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\lang american
so that the dimensionality of the feature space is 
\begin_inset Formula $d=3$
\end_inset

 and larger than the underlying raw datasets 
\begin_inset Formula $2$
\end_inset

