\begin_layout Subsubsection
Pre-Activation 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The preactivation of neuron 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
a_{i}(\mathbf{x}) & =\sum^{d}_{j=1}w_{ij}x_{j}+w_{i0}\\
 & =\mathbf{w}_{i}'\mathbf{x}_{+1},
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{x}_{+1}\in\mathbb{R}^{d+1}$
\end_inset

 denotes the argmentet vector 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbf{x}_{+1}=\begin{bmatrix}1 & x_{1} & \cdots & x_{d}\end{bmatrix}'.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Activation and output 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Applying a (non-linear) activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{i}:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}$
\end_inset

 yields the output of neuron 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
z_{i}(\mathbf{x})=h_{i}(a_{i}(\mathbf{x}))=h_{i}\left(\sum^{d}_{j=1}w_{ij}x_{j}+w_{i0}\right).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That is each neuron 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 has its own weights (and bias) 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{w}\in\mathbb{R}^{d+1}$
\end_inset

 and activation function 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 The latter is usually the same for all neurons except the output-neurons (in the top layer).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Activation functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Linear activation function:
 
\series default
For the network structure to have any reason to exist the activation functions 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{i}$
\end_inset

 must be non-linear.
 If not the neural network 
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathbb{R}^{d}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{K}$
\end_inset

 will simply be a linear function of the inputs aswell.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To see this note that each neuron in the inputlayer would output the following functional form
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
h\left(\sum^{d}_{j=1}w_{ij}x_{j}+w_{i0}\right) & =\sum^{d}_{j=1}\underbrace{aw_{ij}}_{\tilde{w}_{ij}}x_{j}+\underbrace{aw_{i0}+b}_{\tilde{w}_{i0}}\\
 & =\sum^{d}_{j=1}\tilde{w}_{ij}x_{j}+\tilde{w}_{i0},
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and since each pre-activation is simply a affine linear function of the previous affine linear functions all later neurons will output something of the form:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
h\left(a\left(\begin{bmatrix}z_{1} & z_{2} & \cdots & z_{K}\end{bmatrix}\right)\right) & =h\left(\sum^{K}_{k=1}w_{k}z_{k}+w_{0}\right)\\
 & =\sum^{K}_{k=1}\hat{w}_{k}z_{k}+\hat{w}_{0}\\
 & =\sum^{K}_{k=1}\hat{w}_{k}\left(\sum^{d}_{j=1}\tilde{w}_{kj}x_{j}+\tilde{w}_{k0}\right)+\hat{w}_{0}\\
 & =\sum^{K}_{k=1}\hat{w}_{k}\sum^{d}_{j=1}\tilde{w}_{kj}x_{j}+\underbrace{\sum^{K}_{k=1}\hat{w}_{k}\tilde{w}_{k0}+\hat{w}_{0}}_{\bar{w}_{0}}\\
 & =\sum^{K}_{k=1}\sum^{d}_{j=1}\hat{w}_{k}\tilde{w}_{kj}x_{j}+\bar{w}_{0}\\
 & =\sum^{d}_{j=1}\sum^{K}_{k=1}\hat{w}_{k}\tilde{w}_{kj}x_{j}+\bar{w}_{0}\\
 & =\sum^{d}_{j=1}x_{j}\underbrace{\sum^{K}_{k=1}\left(\hat{w}_{k}\tilde{w}_{kj}\right)}_{\bar{w}_{j}}+\bar{w}_{0}\\
 & =\sum^{d}_{j=1}\bar{w}_{j}x_{j}+\bar{w}_{0}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This then also goes for all other layers recieving these outputs (by the exact same argument as above).
 So in summary:
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{i}$
\end_inset

 is linear for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 then there is no purpose to the neural network structure and the simple affine linear model of linear regression fits the model just as well.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Well known activation functions (greatest hits).
 
\series default
Here i list some of the most common activation functions.
 Some are more common for outputlayers for example logistic activation and are rarely used in hidden layers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Step / Threshold
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h(a)=\mathbb{I}(a>0),
\]

\end_inset

with range 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{R}\in\{0,1\}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Hyperbolic tangent
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h(a)=tanh(a),
\]

\end_inset

with range 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{R}\in(-1,1)$
\end_inset

 and with a visual form much like 
\emph on
alternative sigmoid.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Rectified linear unit (ReLU)
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h(a)=\max(0,a),
\]

\end_inset

with range 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{R}[0,\infty)$
\end_inset

.
 Note that there the derivative of the loss function wrt.
 pre-activation weights is uninformative in the regrion 
\begin_inset Formula $a<0$
\end_inset

.
 By that i mean 
\begin_inset Formula $\partial h_{i}/\partial w_{ij}=0$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{w})<0$
\end_inset

 and so it does not help us adjust weights to decrease loss in these negative regions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Fermi / logistic
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h(a)=\frac{1}{1+\exp\left\{ -a\right\} },
\]

\end_inset

with range 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{R}\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Alternative sigmoid
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h(a)=\frac{a}{1+|a|},
\]

\end_inset

with range 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{R}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Exponential LU (ELU,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha>0$
\end_inset

)
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h(a)=\begin{cases}
a & a\geq0\\
\alpha(\exp\{a\}-1) & a<0
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

with range 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{R}\in(-\alpha,\infty)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here the functions are plottet where for ELU 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=1$
\end_inset

 is chosen.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename pictures/activation_funcitons.png
	scale 45

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Gradient descent
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To optimize a neural network with error function 
\begin_inset Formula $E=\sum^{N}_{n=1}E_{n}$
\end_inset

 (as with loglikelihood functions) we introduce gradient descent.
 The bare form of this algorithm is simply
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{w}^{(t+1)} & =\mathbf{w}^{(t)}+\mathbf{\Delta_{w}}^{(t)}\\
 & =\mathbf{w}^{(t)}-\eta\nabla E|_{\mathbf{w}^{(t)}},
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with learning rate 
\begin_inset Formula $\eta>0$
\end_inset

 determining how large steps are in proportion to the gradient.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename pictures/gradient_descent.png
	scale 60

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Often we add a 
\emph on
momentum term
\emph default
 (in an attempt to improve performance) which intuitively works like a heavy ball.
 When a heavy ball has gained momentum from accelerating down a steep hill it will roll over local minimums and further down towards the global minimum (hopefully).
 Formally we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{w}^{(t+1)} & =\mathbf{w}^{(t)}-\eta\nabla E|_{\mathbf{w}^{(t)}}+\mu\mathbf{\Delta_{w}}^{(t-1)}\\
 & =\mathbf{w}^{(t)}-\eta\nabla E|_{\mathbf{w}^{(t)}}+\mu\left(\mathbf{w}^{(t)}-\mathbf{w}^{(t-1)}\right),
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with momentum parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu>0$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The question now really is how to calculate the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\nabla E|_{\mathbf{w}^{(t)}}$
\end_inset

 given by 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial E}{\partial w_{ij}}=\sum^{N}_{n=1}\frac{\partial E_{n}}{\partial w_{ij}},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
for 
\begin_inset Formula $(j,i)\in\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

.
 Recall that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

 contains all the edges connection the output of neuron 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 to the input of neuron 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 which is scaled by 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{ij}$
\end_inset

.
 This is answered in the next section.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Backpropagation 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The goal is to calculate 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial E_{n}}{\partial w_{ij}}$
\end_inset

 for every 
\begin_inset Formula $(j,i)\in\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Foot
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Why not for 
\begin_inset Formula $(j,i)\neq\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

?
 These weights simply do not exist.
 The model only has weights symbolizing the strength of the connect from neuron 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 to neuron 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 There is no connection from 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 back to 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 so this 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{ji}$
\end_inset

 does not exist.
\end_layout

\end_inset

 Applying the chain rule to every element of the error function 
\begin_inset Formula $E=\sum^{N}_{n=1}E_{n}$
\end_inset

 yields the following.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{n}$
\end_inset

 is a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in the network.
 and that any 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a function of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{j}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $(j,i)\in\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore the chain rule yields
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{\partial E_{n}}{\partial w_{ij}} & =\frac{\partial E_{n}}{\partial z_{i}}\frac{\partial z_{i}}{\partial a_{i}}\frac{\partial a_{i}}{\partial w_{ij}}\\
 & =\frac{\partial E_{n}}{\partial z_{i}}h_{i}'(a_{i})\left[\mathbf{z}_{n}\right]_{j}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where the last step uses that 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
z_{i}(\mathbf{z})\equiv h_{i}(a_{i}(\mathbf{z}))\implies\frac{\partial z_{i}}{\partial a_{i}}=h'_{i}(a_{i}),
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{z}\in\mathbb{R}^{M}$
\end_inset

 contains all the inputs from the previous layer to neuron 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 For the last factor we used that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
a_{i}(\mathbf{z}_{n})=w_{i0}+\sum^{M}_{m=1}w_{im}\left[\mathbf{z}_{n}\right]_{m}\implies\frac{\partial a_{i}}{\partial w_{ij}}=\left[\mathbf{z}_{n}\right]_{j}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore the missing factor to be calculated is 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial E_{n}}{\partial z_{i}}$
\end_inset

 which we will now turn to.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
The ouput layer
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the output layer 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial E_{n}}{\partial z_{i}}$
\end_inset

 can be directly calculated.
 To see this let the loss function be the sum of squared errors 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
E=\frac{1}{2}\sum^{N}_{n=1}||f(\mathbf{x}_{n};\mathbf{w})-\mathbf{y}_{n}||^{2} & =\frac{1}{2}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\left(\sqrt{\sum^{K}_{k=1}\left(\left[f(\mathbf{x}_{n};\mathbf{w})\right]_{k}-\left[\mathbf{y}_{n}\right]_{k}\right)^{2}}\right)^{2}\\
 & =\frac{1}{2}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\sum^{K}_{k=1}\left(\left[f(\mathbf{x}_{n};\mathbf{w})\right]_{k}-\left[\mathbf{y}_{n}\right]_{k}\right)^{2}\\
 & =\frac{1}{2}\sum^{N}_{n=1}\sum^{K}_{k=1}\left(\left[\mathbf{z}_{n}\right]_{k}-\left[\mathbf{y}_{n}\right]_{k}\right)^{2}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
for 
\begin_inset Formula $k\in V_{output}$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore we have (for 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in V_{output}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(j,i)\in\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

)
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{\partial E_{n}}{\partial z_{i}} & =\frac{\partial}{\partial z_{i}}\frac{1}{2}\sum^{K}_{k=1}\left(\left[\mathbf{z}_{n}\right]_{k}-\left[\mathbf{y}_{n}\right]_{k}\right)^{2}\\
 & =\left[\mathbf{z}_{n}\right]_{i}-\left[\mathbf{y}_{n}\right]_{i}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{i}(a_{i})=a_{i}\implies h_{i}'(a_{i})=1$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in V_{output}$
\end_inset

 we thus have 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{\partial E}{\partial w_{ij}} & =\sum^{N}_{n=1}\frac{\partial E_{n}}{\partial z_{i}}h_{i}'(a_{i})z_{j}\\
 & =\sum^{N}_{n=1}\frac{\partial E_{n}}{\partial z_{i}}\left[\mathbf{z}_{n}\right]_{j}\\
 & =\sum^{N}_{n=1}\left(\left[\mathbf{z}_{n}\right]_{i}-\left[\mathbf{y}_{n}\right]_{i}\right)\left[\mathbf{z}_{n}\right]_{j}
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Hidden layers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For hidden layers we apply the apply the chainrule to obtain
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
\frac{\partial E}{\partial z_{j}} & =\sum_{k\text{ in }(j,k)\in\mathcal{A}}\frac{\partial E}{\partial z_{k}}\frac{\partial z_{k}}{\partial z_{j}}\\
 & =\sum_{k\text{ in }(j,k)\in\mathcal{A}}\frac{\partial E}{\partial z_{k}}\frac{\partial z_{k}}{\partial a_{k}}\frac{\partial a_{k}}{\partial z_{j}}\\
 & =\sum_{k\text{ in }(j,k)\in\mathcal{A}}\frac{\partial E}{\partial z_{k}}h_{k}'(a_{k})w_{kj}.
\end{align*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The partial derivatives of the layer just before 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{ouput}$
\end_inset

 is then calculated by pluging in 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial E}{\partial z_{k}}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $k\in V_{output}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $(j,k)\in\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

.
 Then the third layers uses the second layers calculated 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\partial E}{\partial z_{j}}$
\end_inset

 and so on.
 This is sometimes referred to as the backwards pass.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Deep Learning
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Convolution / cross-correlation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A convolution is a filtering transformation applied to image-like data.
 Image-like in the sence that datapoints close to each other in the dataset structure somehow relate to each other - like two pixels are closely related in a picture.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Formally let 
\begin_inset Formula $C(i,j)$
\end_inset

 be the output image from applying a convolution operation (or cross correlation operation) to the pixel in image 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 at location 
\begin_inset Formula $(i,j)$
\end_inset

.
 Additionally 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 takes a kernel matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 as input.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Cross correlation
\series default
:
 It is intuitive and defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{corr}(i,j)=\sum_{m\in\mathcal{M}}\sum_{n\in\mathcal{N}}K(m,n)\cdot I(i+m,j+n),
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where the kernel for example could be the vertical edge 
\emph on
filter kernel
\emph default
:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
K=\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1\\
-1 & 1
\end{bmatrix},
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
and thus 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}=\{0,1\}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{N}=\{0,1\}.$
\end_inset

 So basically for each pixel we overlay the filter kernel 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

,
 weigh the surronding pixels accordingly and sum.
 An example is given here:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename pictures/Crosscorrelation_example.png
	scale 50

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Convolution
\series default
:
 Essentially the same method but where we first flip 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 above 
\begin_inset Formula $180$
\end_inset

-degrees,
 shiftet so the bottom right is at the pixel 
\begin_inset Formula $(i,j)$
\end_inset

 and then apply cross correlation.
 The formula is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
C_{conv}(i,j)=(I*K)(i,j)=\sum_{m\in\mathcal{M}}\sum_{n\in\mathcal{N}}K(m,n)\cdot I(i-m,j-n).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note the shift dependents on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{M}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{N}$
\end_inset

.
 For example there is no shift for symmetrical convolutions 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{M}=\mathcal{N}=\{-k,-(k-1),\dots,0,\dots,(k-1),k\}.
\]

\end_inset

(Remember this is all in comparison with cross-correlation).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Padding
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Convolution layers or cross correlation layers reduce the number of pixels.
 For example a 3 by 3 cross correlation will remove 2 pixels in each dimension thus reducing an image from 
\begin_inset Formula $(M,N)$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $(M-2,N-2$
\end_inset

) to avoid this one can add 
\emph on
padding 
\emph default
around the input image 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 so that it becomes 
\begin_inset Formula $(M+2,N+2)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The padding values are usually just zeros (zero padding) since the specific values rarely matters in practice.
 But it could also be some notion of mean values.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename pictures/padding.png
	scale 50

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Equivariance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If the input (for example a traffic sign in a picture) is translated / moved then the output (i.e.
 the feature space of the neurons in a particular layer) will translate in the same way.
 This is why convolutional neural networks are particularly relevant for image recognition tasks since it usually does not matter where in the frame a person or item is located.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Pooling layer
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Pooling layers reduce dimensionality by for example looping over all non-intersecting 
\begin_inset Formula $2\times2$
\end_inset

 grids on the feature image with a 
\emph on
stride 
\emph default
of 2.
 Then applying the 
\begin_inset Formula $\max\{\cdot\}$
\end_inset

 operator over those four values as the output.
 Thus in this example a 4 by 4 feature map is reduces to 2 by 2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename pictures/pooling.png
	scale 50

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Invariance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Invariance refers to operations that does not change its output when the input is translated / shiftet.
 Pooling layers can help with invariance which can be understood through an example.
 Let the kernel detect cat-shapes.
 Then translating the cat 5 pixels to the right translates the feature map equivaraintly 5 pixels to the right (remember that the feature space is the output of the cross-correlation using the cat-kernel).
 Applying a max pooling layer will still just indicate 
\begin_inset Formula $\texttt{True}$
\end_inset

 there is a cat.
 Thus the algorithm is 
\emph on
invariant
\emph default
 to translations of the cat.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename pictures/invariance.png
	scale 50

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Receptive field
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Nerons in deeper layers have a greater 
\emph on
effective receptive 
\emph default
field than its kernel would suggest.
 This is simply because the feature space the neuron percieves comes from a poolin layer which have condensed larger field.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename pictures/receptive_field.png
	scale 60

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Example:
 VGG-16 architecture
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As a quick recap I will explain the general details of the VGG-16 architecture depicted here.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename pictures/example_CNN_architecture.png
	scale 60

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The input layer is one RGB image - so there is 3 input channels corresponding to red,
 green and blue respectively.
 Each input channel is of size 
\begin_inset Formula $244\times244$
\end_inset

.
 The convolutional layer outputs 64 feature maps i.e.
 there are 64 kernels and biases needing to be trained in this single layer.
 Between each layer the non-linear ReLU activation function is applied.
 Each 
\begin_inset Formula $224\times224$
\end_inset

 feature-map is then reduced by max pooling to 
\begin_inset Formula $122\times122$
\end_inset

 and convolutional layers now take the 64 features and double them and creating 128 feature maps (thus and additional 128 kernels and biases needs training for each layer).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the end,
 we obtain 512 feature maps,
 each of size 
\begin_inset Formula $7\times7$
\end_inset

.
 These are flattened into a single vector containing 
\begin_inset Formula $7\cdot7\cdot512=25,088$
\end_inset

 features,
 which serves as input to a fully connected layer with 4096 neurons.
 This is followed by another fully connected layer with 4096 neurons and finally an output layer with 1,000 neurons,
 corresponding to the 1000 possible classification categories.
 A softmax activation is applied to the final output to obtain a probability distribution over these categories.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Tricks-of-the-Trade
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Ensemble Methods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Ensemble methods combat overfitting through the 
\emph on
cancellation of errors effect
\emph default
 and has proven to be very succesfull in the CNN setting.
 CNN ensembles can be created over the same datasets because of the importance of random initialization on the local minimum obtained.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Reminder:
 cancellation of errors effect
\series default
:
 When individual ensemble members perform better than random guessing and make independent errors,
 their errors average out and the combined model outperforms the individual predictors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Dropout
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A heuristic method for avoiding overfitting is 
\emph on
dropout
\emph default
.
 It is an ensemble method where each member of the ensemble is a subnetwork where randomly a binary mask 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbf{R}$
\end_inset

 has been applied to remove neurons.
 Then training commences on all the subnetworks individually to create the ensemble.
 The two right models in the illustration would be two members of the whole ensemble.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename pictures/dropout.png
	scale 50

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Residual Leraning / Shorcuts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Yet another method to combat local minima is shortcuts.
 Where in each layer the input data directly passes trough as illustrated below.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename pictures/shotcuts.png
	scale 50

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This can drastically smooth the error surface.
 This example visualizes the error surface (loss vs.
 parameters) for a network with 56 layers without (a) and with (b) shortcuts.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename pictures/smooth_error_surface.png
	scale 50

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Augmentation og Invariance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Data augmentation refers to applying transformations to the input data that do not change their labels.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $T=\{𝑇1,…,\}$
\end_inset

 be a set of transformations.
 During training,
 for each
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
element added to a mini-batch it is decided if and which transformations are applied to it.
 In this way,
 we learn invariance with respect to the transformations in 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

.
 If augmentation is always applied,
 the network hardly see exactly the same input twice.
 Note that the “new” training examples are not i.i.d.
 samples!
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Examples of transformations for images could be cropping,
 rotating,
 flipping,
 blurring,
 adding noise,
 \SpecialChar ldots

\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Batch and Layer Normalization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Batch normalization and layer normalization normalize intermediate neuron activations across different dimensions.
 This can stabilize gradient propagation,
 improve the conditioning of the optimization problem,
 and allow gradient descent to converge more reliably and often faster.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Batch normalization normalizes 
\emph on
each 
\emph default
neurons activation over 
\emph on
multiple 
\emph default
observations (i.e.
 the batch).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Layer normalization normalizes neuron activation 
\emph on
layer 
\emph default
for 
\emph on
each 
\emph default
observation.
\end_layout

