\backslash
subsubsection{Why might a linear projection be a problem?}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
PCA works well if the data lie close to a low-dimensional plane (or the higher-dimensional equivalent of a plane).
 But what if the data lie on a curved surface?
 Consider the Swiss roll on slides~7--8.
 Imagine a sheet of paper rolled up in 3D.
 We need three coordinates to write down the position of a point on the roll,
 but if we unroll the paper we only need two coordinates to locate that same point.
 I.e.
 the 
\backslash
emph{ambient dimension} is $D=3$ and the 
\backslash
emph{intrinsic dimension} is $d=2$.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Now imagine projecting the roll onto a plane.
 Points on different layers of the roll can end up on top of each other,
 even if we would have to walk a long way along the paper to get from one to the other.
 This is the issue with using a single linear projection.
 What we would like to do in this example is something more like unrolling the paper.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The 
\backslash
emph{manifold assumption} is that the data lie on (or close to) a low-dimensional surface inside $
\backslash
mathbb{R}^{D}$.
 A $d$-dimensional manifold locally looks like $
\backslash
mathbb{R}^{d}$.
 By locally I mean that if we zoom in on a small patch of the Swiss roll it looks like a piece of a plane,
 even though the whole thing is curved.
 Manifold learning tries to recover or approximate this underlying structure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Another example from the slides is ethanol.
 The molecule has 9 atoms and we can describe one configuration using $D=9
\backslash
cdot3=27$ coordinates.
 However,
 much of the variation in the configurations can be described by two rotation angles.
 Each angle wraps around,
 which gives the approximate torus (doughnut shape) on slide~10.
 So there can be a big difference between how many numbers we use to record the data and how many numbers we need to describe the main ways it varies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

