\backslash
subsection{t-SNE}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
subsubsection{The crowding problem}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Why change symmetric SNE?
 Imagine a point at the origin in 2D with neighbors at $(1,0)$,
 $(-1,0)$,
 $(0,1)$ and $(0,-1)$.
 All four neighbors are distance 1 from the origin,
 and they have room to be separated from each other.
 Now try to arrange them on a line.
 There are only two positions at distance 1 from the origin!
 So we have to put some neighbors on top of each other or change some of the distances.
 This is the 
\backslash
emph{crowding problem}.
 There are fewer directions to spread out in when we go down in dimension.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
With the Gaussian weights in $Y$,
 affinities fall very quickly as we move points apart.
 So even pairs that are only moderately similar can end up competing for space close together in the embedding.
 The idea of t-SNE is to give such points more room by using a weight that falls more slowly with distance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
subsubsection{What changes in t-SNE?}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
We calculate the same $P$ from the original data and minimize the same KL divergence.
 The change is how we calculate $Q(Y)$.
 The new weights are
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
[
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
q_{ij}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
frac{(1+
\backslash
ell_{ij}^{2})^{-1}}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
{
\backslash
displaystyle
\backslash
sum_{k=1}^{N}
\backslash
sum_{l
\backslash
neq k}(1+
\backslash
ell_{kl}^{2})^{-1}},
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
qquad i
\backslash
neq j,
\backslash
qquad q_{ii}=0.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The weight $(1+
\backslash
ell_{ij}^{2})^{-1}$ comes from the Student $t$-distribution with one degree of freedom (also called the Cauchy distribution).
 Hence the name 
\backslash
emph{t-distributed Stochastic Neighbor Embedding}.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
What does the heavier tail actually do?
 At distance $
\backslash
ell_{ij}=3$ the old weight is $e^{-9}
\backslash
approx0.00012$ and the new weight is $1/(1+9)=0.1$ (both before normalization).
 I.e.
 the new weight can still be appreciable at distances where the Gaussian weight is already very close to zero.
 This lets the embedding put moderately similar points further apart while still assigning them some affinity.
 Close neighbors can stay close and different groups get more room to spread out.
 The optimization is again gradient descent with momentum,
 with the gradient now calculated using the new $Q(Y)$.
\end_layout

