\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
subsubsection{MNIST}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Each observation is a $28
\backslash
times28$ grayscale picture of a handwritten digit,
 so $D=784$ and $N=70{,}000$.
 In the example on slides~27 and~35--36,
 PCA first reduces the data from 784 to 50 dimensions.
 Then SNE or t-SNE takes those 50 coordinates down to 2 (with perplexity 50 in this example).
 So PCA and t-SNE can actually be used in the same pipeline.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The symmetric SNE plot already shows some structure,
 but several of the digit groups run into each other.
 In the t-SNE plot the groups are easier to tell apart.
 Note that the digit labels are used to color and annotate the plot afterwards.
 The embedding itself is unsupervised.
 Each dot is an image,
 which is easier to see on slide~40 where some of the digits are drawn at their positions in the embedding.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
subsubsection{Gaussian mixture,
 Swiss roll and spiral}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
begin{itemize}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
    
\backslash
item 
\backslash
textbf{Gaussian mixture (slide~37).} The data come from four Gaussian distributions with the same covariance matrix.
 The PCA plot already shows the four groups and the t-SNE plot separates them more clearly in this example.
 The gaps in the t-SNE plot describe the layout of the embedding.
 To compare how far apart the Gaussians actually are we would go back to the original data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
    
\backslash
item 
\backslash
textbf{Swiss roll (slide~38).} The PCA projection still looks like a roll viewed from the side.
 The t-SNE plot keeps many of the nearby colors together but breaks the roll into separate pieces.
 So even when t-SNE does a good job with local neighbors,
 it can change how the whole surface is connected.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
    
\backslash
item 
\backslash
textbf{Spiral (slide~39).} The PCA projection still shows the oscillations of the two spirals and makes them cross in the plot.
 t-SNE lays them out as two nearly parallel curves.
 The interesting thing here is which points stay next to each other along each curve.
 The shape itself changes quite a lot.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
end{itemize}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
subsubsection{The home assignment example}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The original data on slide~41 look like two linked rings in 3D.
 The two PCA plots on slide~43 show how the projection changes when we normalize the input.
 In the t-SNE plots the two rings are visually separated for both perplexities.
 At perplexity 30 the rings look rougher and broken up in places,
 while at perplexity 400 they look much smoother.
 This is a good example of what changing the perplexity does.
 With 400 the affinities around each point are spread over a much larger neighborhood,
 so the algorithm is trying to preserve similarities over a broader part of the data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
subsection{How to read a t-SNE plot}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
The first thing I would look at is which observations end up near each other.
 The axes can be rotated or translated without changing any of the pairwise distances,
 so the axis values themselves have no particular meaning.
 The sizes of groups and the gaps between them can also change with the perplexity and the optimization.
 For example a large empty gap might come from the way t-SNE arranged the points in 2D.
 The Swiss roll is a nice reminder of this since we know it started out as one continuous sheet.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Recall that clustering gives us a group assignment for each point,
 while t-SNE gives us a position for each point.
 To use them together as on slide~45 we can run e.g.
 $k$-means on $X$,
 fit a 2D t-SNE embedding and then color each $
\backslash
mathbf{y}_{i}$ by the cluster assigned to $
\backslash
mathbf{x}_{i}$.
 This lets us see how the cluster assignments line up with the neighborhoods in the plot.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
One practical detail is that the exact method works with all pairs of observations.
 So for a fixed output dimension the work per iteration grows like $N^{2}$.
 The MNIST example uses an approximate implementation,
 which makes it possible to use the method on a dataset of that size.
\end_layout

